#!/usr/bin/env -S bashy --bashsharp
# A skill that names its own tasks.md explicitly, via `metadata.tasks:` —
# the same pointer convention `skills run --target` already resolves (never
# an implicit scan of whatever tasks.md the caller's directory happens to
# carry, which would graft methods onto an unrelated skill, see skill.bsh
# sharing this directory). The alias answers `methods` from the skill's own
# three verbs (`verify`, `probe`, `run`) PLUS one method per tasks.md
# target — `send()` here, from the `### send` heading's own `Effects:`
# line. Each is `bashy skill run skill --target <name>` under the hood, so
# calling `s.send()` and running `bashy skill run skill --target send` by
# hand agree on stdout and status. A skill with no `tasks:` pointer keeps
# only the three verbs — see skill.bsh, which sits beside this same
# tasks.md and stays untasked precisely because it never names it.

~~~skill as s
---
name: skill
description: Greet whoever is reading, verifiably.
metadata:
  tasks: tasks.md
---
# skill

Say hello. That is the whole procedure. Its tasks.md carries one step,
`send`, that this script calls as a method.
~~~

out := s.send()
echo "send: $out"

@guard("read")
func rehearse() {
  x := s.send()
  echo "rehearsal: status=$? (a read-only guard denies a net target)"
}
rehearse()
